# Mutation cases for "a broadcast log orders every slot hand-over".
#
# Run with
# `scripts/mutate sce-build/tests/mutations/a_broadcast_log_orders_every_slot_hand_over.cases`.
#
# The bounded broadcast log of ADR 0062 hands each entry from the one publisher to
# any number of readers through a sequence-lock: a stamp that carries the entry's
# sequence number, relaxed atomic words between two fences, and a head index. Each
# order the protocol needs is a decision the TLA+ model (`docs/formal/BroadcastLog.tla`)
# cannot check, because it is sequentially consistent. Two orders the code keeps are not
# needed and have no case, each with its reason where it would stand. The oracle is the
# loom models in `backends/rust/forge-runtime/tests/loom_broadcast_log.rs`, which
# explore every interleaving and every outcome the memory model allows, so a weakened
# order is not something they might sample but something they must reach.
#
# The protocol is written once, generic over the width of the stamp, in
# `broadcast_log_core.rs`; the 64-bit row these models run is its instantiation over
# `Wide`. The narrow row's own decisions (the head read modulo the stamp space, the
# truncation of the stamp, the test for a young log) are in the casefile
# `a_narrow_broadcast_log_reads_its_head_modulo_the_stamp_space.cases`, which runs the
# models over a stamp of three bits.
#
# Two of the cases are not hypothetical: the first version of the log stored the
# `busy` stamp relaxed and loaded the second stamp relaxed, and each was found by
# the model `two_readers_each_hold_their_own_cursor` as a lag that skipped nothing.
# The sequentially consistent model had passed both.
#
# The models build only under `--cfg loom`. The selector passes it as a cargo
# `--config` rather than through RUSTFLAGS, so the casefile carries the whole
# command, and gives the build a target directory of its own so that the cfg does
# not invalidate the ordinary build's.
#
# Anchors are literal slices of the source; an edit that moves one is reported
# INCONCLUSIVE with the anchor quoted, never as a pass.

mutation_tests -p sce-forge-runtime --release --target-dir target/loom --config 'build.rustflags=["--cfg","loom"]' --test loom_broadcast_log
mutation_targets backends/rust/forge-runtime/src/broadcast_log_core.rs
mutation_oracles backends/rust/forge-runtime/tests/loom_broadcast_log.rs

# ── The publisher's three needed orders ────────────────────────────

# A reader that sees this stamp and reloads `head` must find the head the previous
# publish stored.
mutation_case "the publisher stores the busy stamp without releasing the head before it" <<'PY'
edit("backends/rust/forge-runtime/src/broadcast_log_core.rs",
     r'''            BroadcastLog::<W, SLOTS, L>::busy(n),
            Ordering::Release,''',
     r'''            BroadcastLog::<W, SLOTS, L>::busy(n),
            Ordering::Relaxed,''')
PY

# Without the fence the busy stamp may reach a reader after the words it guards, and
# a copy that mixes two entries then validates.
mutation_case "the publisher writes the words without a release fence after the busy stamp" <<'PY'
edit("backends/rust/forge-runtime/src/broadcast_log_core.rs",
     r'''        fence(Ordering::Release);''',
     r'''        /* no release fence */''')
PY

# Not a case: the done stamp's release order. The first round of CI found a relaxed
# done stamp survived all three models, and it is not observable. The words of an
# entry reach a reader through the release store of `head` and the acquire load that
# opens `read`, which is the case below; and a reader that learns of a later lap
# from a done stamp is ordered after the `head` store that ended the publish before
# by the release fence, which every store after it carries to an acquire load that
# reads it. Nothing reads a slot's words by way of the done stamp alone. The code
# keeps the release: it costs nothing the protocol notices and it is the order a
# reader of the slot alone would need.

mutation_case "the publisher moves the head without releasing the slot" <<'PY'
edit("backends/rust/forge-runtime/src/broadcast_log_core.rs",
     r'''L::store(&self.log.head.0, next, Ordering::Release);''',
     r'''L::store(&self.log.head.0, next, Ordering::Relaxed);''')
PY

# ── The reader's four needed orders ─────────────────────────────────

mutation_case "a reader loads the head without acquiring the slot behind it" <<'PY'
edit("backends/rust/forge-runtime/src/broadcast_log_core.rs",
     r'''        let head = L::load(&self.head.0, Ordering::Acquire);
        let behind = Self::distance(head, r);''',
     r'''        let head = L::load(&self.head.0, Ordering::Relaxed);
        let behind = Self::distance(head, r);''')
PY

mutation_case "a reader loads the first stamp without acquiring" <<'PY'
edit("backends/rust/forge-runtime/src/broadcast_log_core.rs",
     r'''let first = L::load(&slot.stamp, Ordering::Acquire);''',
     r'''let first = L::load(&slot.stamp, Ordering::Relaxed);''')
PY

mutation_case "a reader copies the words without an acquire fence before the second stamp" <<'PY'
edit("backends/rust/forge-runtime/src/broadcast_log_core.rs",
     r'''            fence(Ordering::Acquire);''',
     r'''            /* no acquire fence */''')
PY

# The load that decides whether the copy was overwritten is also the one a lap
# is read from; a fence orders only the reads before it.
mutation_case "a reader loads the second stamp without acquiring" <<'PY'
edit("backends/rust/forge-runtime/src/broadcast_log_core.rs",
     r'''if L::load(&slot.stamp, Ordering::Acquire) == first {''',
     r'''if L::load(&slot.stamp, Ordering::Relaxed) == first {''')
PY

# Not a case: the acquire order of the reload of `head` after a mismatch. It
# survived the first round of CI and is not observable. Nothing is read after the
# reload: the cursor moves to the oldest retained entry and the lag is counted from
# the value alone. The value is no lower than the one the reader needs because the
# stamp it just saw (an acquire load, the cases above) orders it after the `head`
# store that ended the publish before the lap, and a load of one location cannot
# return a value older than one that is ordered before it. The code keeps the
# acquire, as it is the order every other load of `head` has.

# ── What the stamp and the window mean ─────────────────────────────

# A stamp compared as a flag cannot tell the entry the reader wants from the one a
# later lap wrote into the same slot.
mutation_case "a reader accepts any written stamp instead of the sequence it wants" <<'PY'
edit("backends/rust/forge-runtime/src/broadcast_log_core.rs",
     r'''        if first == Self::done(r) {''',
     r'''        if first != L::UNWRITTEN {''')
PY

# `RETENTION + 1` slots mean an entry `SLOTS` behind the head is outside the
# window; one more slot's worth is read as retained while it is being overwritten.
mutation_case "a reader reads an entry a whole ring behind the head as retained" <<'PY'
edit("backends/rust/forge-runtime/src/broadcast_log_core.rs",
     r'''        if behind >= SLOTS as u64 {''',
     r'''        if behind > SLOTS as u64 {''')
PY
